      \field{abstract}{While modern NLP models are adept at handling syntactically rich prose and sentence structure, they still struggle with the flexible problem solving and general reasoning ability that humans exhibit. In this paper, we apply diverse NLP methods to one example of a semantically complex reasoning task, The New York Times Connections game. We explore baseline methods that are specific to the semantic grouping task, such as K-Means clustering on various word embeddings. Then, we benchmark in-context learning on a large LLM as well as finetune a smaller LLM using existing examples of solved puzzles. We also explore unique methods of introducing complex linguistic and metalinguistic reasoning capabilities of a larger LLM into a smaller LLM, through methods like fine-tuning with distilled rationales and Verbal Reinforcement Learning. We found promising results with Verbal RL, as the smaller model was able to absorb critique from a larger language model and improve on previous answers, similar to how human solvers approach language puzzles.}
